    "response": "The short answer is **no, you should not make anything of the fact that the panel was entirely women, nor is it a sign of bias or irregularity.** \n\nHere is a breakdown of why that is, what is more likely at play, and how you should approach the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel's Composition Is Unlikely to Signify Anything\n* **Demographics in Public Policy:** In many government ministries and civil service departments, women represent a significant portion—often a majority—of senior policy and HR leadership. An all-female panel of four is statistically common in modern public administration.\n* **Both You and the Appointee Are Women:** Since both you and the successful candidate are women, there is no evident gender-based preference at play. \n* **Panel Composition Rules:** Civil service panels are typically assembled based on relevant roles (e.g., the hiring director, an HR representative, a policy peer, and an external/independent assessor) and completion of recruitment training, rather than engineered gender quotas. Unless there is a specific internal rule in your jurisdiction requiring mixed-gender panels, an all-female panel is standard and procedurally sound.\n\nRaising or dwelling on the panel's gender composition is unlikely to help you and could be perceived as looking for grievance where none exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What More Likely Determined the Outcome\nWhen a competition reaches a final shortlist of three after two rounds of interviews, **all finalists are already deemed qualified on paper.** At that stage, selection almost always hinges on:\n\n* **Specific Strategic Fit:** The announcement highlighted that the successful candidate had *\"experience of leading international projects.\"* If the ministry is expanding its multilateral work, international engagement, or cross-border coordination, that specific background may have aligned more closely with the department’s immediate priorities than domestic civil service tenure or legal policy expertise.\n* **Interview Scoring and Competencies:** Civil service competitions typically use standardized competency scoring (e.g., leadership, stakeholder management, strategic vision). A candidate who performs slightly better against the specific scoring rubrics on the day will get the nod, even if another candidate has more total years of service or additional degrees.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\nTwo weeks without a written response is very common in the public sector:\n* **HR Formalities:** Official written feedback often requires the panel chair’s notes to be compiled, validated by HR, and held until the formal offer and pre-employment checks for the successful candidate are finalized.\n* **Bureaucratic Pace:** Panel members have their normal day jobs to resume once interviews finish, and drafting formal candidate feedback is often delayed behind operational work.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a Polite Follow-Up:** \n   After two weeks, a brief, professional check-in with HR or the panel chair is entirely appropriate. \n   > *\"I hope you are well. Following up on my request from [Date], I am writing to see if written feedback on my interview for the Senior Policy Adviser role is available. I found the interview process very constructive and would greatly value the panel’s insights to support my continued development.\"*\n\n2. **Offer to Take Verbal Feedback:** \n   Panel chairs often dread drafting formal written feedback due to time constraints, but are frequently happy to offer a **10-to-15-minute phone or video debrief**. Offering this option can get you answers much faster.\n\n3. **Ask Targeted Questions:**\n   When you do receive feedback, focus on actionable points:\n   * *“What distinguished the successful candidate’s responses from mine?”*\n   * *“Were there specific areas where my experience—such as international project leadership—was seen as a gap for this specific role?”*\n   * *“What would make my profile more competitive for similar roles in the future?”*\n\nMaking the final three for a senior policy role proves that your qualifications (law degree, MPP, and 10 years of service) are well-respected. Focus on obtaining constructive feedback on competencies and project experience rather than the panel’s gender makeup.\n",
